  "theorem_name": "GoldbachP2LiouvilleParitySaturationEquivalence",
  "declared_proposition": "Let J(n) indicate that n is prime or a composite semiprime and let lambda(n)=(-1)^Omega(n). On the support of J, the prime indicator is J(n)(1-lambda(n))/2. For even N define C(N)=sum_p J(N-p) and L(N)=sum_p J(N-p)lambda(N-p), with p prime. Then R(N)=(C(N)-L(N))/2 and S(N)=(C(N)+L(N))/2 exactly. At every N with C(N)>0, the TICKET-200 semiprime-only condition is equivalent to L(N)=C(N), while Goldbach positivity is equivalent to the strict parity defect L(N)<C(N).",
  "mathematical_argument": "A prime has Omega=1 and lambda=-1; a composite semiprime has Omega=2 and lambda=+1. Therefore J(1-lambda)/2 and J(1+lambda)/2 are the exact prime and composite-semiprime projectors. Summing after fixing the first prime gives C=R+S and L=S-R, hence the two displayed identities. Since C-L=2R, R=0 iff L=C and R>0 iff L<C.",
  "reproducible_computation": {
    "theorem": "Let J(n) indicate that n is prime or a composite semiprime and let lambda(n)=(-1)^Omega(n). On the support of J, the prime indicator is J(n)(1-lambda(n))/2. For even N define C(N)=sum_p J(N-p) and L(N)=sum_p J(N-p)lambda(N-p), with p prime. Then R(N)=(C(N)-L(N))/2 and S(N)=(C(N)+L(N))/2 exactly. At every N with C(N)>0, the TICKET-200 semiprime-only condition is equivalent to L(N)=C(N), while Goldbach positivity is equivalent to the strict parity defect L(N)<C(N).",
    "proof": "A prime has Omega=1 and lambda=-1; a composite semiprime has Omega=2 and lambda=+1. Therefore J(1-lambda)/2 and J(1+lambda)/2 are the exact prime and composite-semiprime projectors. Summing after fixing the first prime gives C=R+S and L=S-R, hence the two displayed identities. Since C-L=2R, R=0 iff L=C and R>0 iff L<C.",
